U.S. smartphone shipments fell 9% year-over-year in the third quarter of 2026, and yet Apple grew its share of the market from 50% to 57%, according to the latest U.S. Market Monitor data from <a href="https://counterpointresearch.com/en/insights/us-smartphone-shipments-fell-9-percent-yoy-as-memory-costs-squeeze-android">Counterpoint Research</a>. Apple was the only major manufacturer to avoid a shipment decline in the quarter.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/10/iPhone-18-Pro-Pre-Orders-Feature-Sans-Text.jpg" alt="" width="2500" height="1406" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1071763" /><br/>
Counterpoint put the wider market decline down to rising memory costs, which hit Android manufacturers hardest at the low end. Thin margins leave little room for these vendors to absorb higher component prices without raising their prices or cutting volume.<br/>
<br/>
Samsung held its ground at 25% share, up one point year-over-year, thanks to its flagship Galaxy S and Z series devices and the low-end Galaxy A-series. But it was Motorola that took the biggest hit – its share fell four points to 8% as memory costs squeezed its large low-end lineup, essentially reversing the share growth it posted in the second quarter. Meanwhile, Google's Pixel 11 launch was not strong enough to prevent a slight decline in share.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/11/us-smartphone-shipments-share-counterpoint-q3-2026.jpg" alt="" width="1200" height="558" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1079705" /><br/>
Smartphone sales at the Big Three carriers already declined 4% year-over-year in Q3, and Counterpoint similarly expects weak Q4 sales as consumers increasingly time purchases around the best deals.<br/>
<br/>
